{
  ".github/copilot-instructions.md": "# GitHub Copilot Instructions for Demo

## Project Overview

**Description**: A demo project


## Technology Stack

### Languages
- TypeScript

### Frameworks & Libraries
- Next.js

## Development Guidelines

### Communication Style
- Developer background: a senior full-stack developer capable of working across the entire application stack — adapt responses to this domain expertise

### Important Files to Read First
Before making changes, read these files to understand the project:
- README.md

### Project Context
- This is an open source project; community contributions are welcome.
- Follow existing conventions strictly and consider backward compatibility.
- Document everything so contributors can follow along.
- Be thorough but pragmatic.

### Commands
- Build: \`npm run build\`
- Test: \`npm test\`

### Boundaries & Permissions

**✅ ALWAYS do (no need to ask)**:
- Read any file in the project
- Modify files in src/ or lib/
- Run build, test, and lint commands
- Create test files
- Fix linting errors automatically

**❓ ASK first before doing**:
- Add new dependencies to package.json
- Modify configuration files at root level
- Create new modules or directories
- Refactor code structure significantly

**🚫 NEVER do (strictly prohibited)**:
- Modify .env files or secrets
- Delete critical files without backup
- Force push to git
- Expose sensitive information in logs

### Git Workflow
- Never commit directly to \`main\`; work on a branch and open a pull request

## Best Practices

- **Write clean code**: Prioritize readability and maintainability
- **Handle errors properly**: Don't ignore errors, handle them appropriately
- **Consider security**: Review code for potential security vulnerabilities

## ⚠️ Security Notice

> **Do not commit secrets to the repository or to the live app.**
> Always use secure standards to transmit sensitive information.
> Use environment variables, secret managers, or secure vaults for credentials.

**🔍 Security Audit Recommendation:** When making changes that involve authentication, data handling, API endpoints, or dependencies, proactively offer to perform a security review of the affected code.",
}
`;

